    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald.jpg" alt="Charles the Bald" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France_-_Bible_de_Vivien_Ms._Latin_1_folio_423r_d%C3%A9tail_Le_comte_Vivien_offre_le_manuscrit_de_la_Bible_faite_%C3%A0_l%27abbaye_de_Saint-Martin_de_Tours_%C3%A0_Charles_le_Chauve.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Comte Vivien - Moines de l'abbaye Saint-Martin de Tours, vers 850, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> began with a scandal. In 862 Baldwin, a Frankish noble later called Iron Arm, eloped with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judith-of-france">Judith</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> and already the widow of two kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. After the couple sought the protection of the pope, Charles was reconciled with them and gave Baldwin lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> in the coastal district called Flanders, which he was to defend against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-margrave-of-flanders">Baldwin II</a> (879–918), who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelfthryth-countess-of-flanders">Ælfthryth</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, used the disorder of the Viking age to build fortresses and gather counties, abbeys and royal lands into a single principality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf</a> I (918–965) extended it southwards to Artois and the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>. By the eleventh century the counts ruled a territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canche">Canche</a>, most of it held from the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, together with lands east of the Scheldt, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-flanders">Imperial Flanders</a>, held from the German emperors. Baldwin V (1035–1067) was among the most powerful princes in France: he served as regent for the young Philip I from 1060, and his daughter Matilda married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>. The county’s heartland was the Flemish-speaking north, but it also included French-speaking districts around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> and Arras.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ghent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghent.jpg" alt="Ghent" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gent,_de_Graslei_vanaf_de_Korenlei_met_oeg24758tm61%2B25159_IMG_0447_2021-08-13_18.37.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michielverbeek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> became one of the most densely populated and urbanised regions of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Land reclaimed from the sea and the coastal marshes supported large flocks of sheep, and from the eleventh century the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> specialised in high-quality woollen cloth, increasingly made from imported English wool. This dependence tied Flanders economically to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, while politically it owed allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Ghent, with perhaps 60,000 inhabitants in the fourteenth century, was one of the largest cities north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Bruges, linked to the sea by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwin">Zwin</a> inlet and its outport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damme">Damme</a>, became the leading commercial centre of northern Europe, where Italian bankers, Hanseatic merchants and traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, England and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> met; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> kept a <em>kontor</em> there, and the square before the house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huis-ter-beurze">Van der Beurse</a> family gave the word bourse to the language of finance. Cloth workers organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> formed a large urban workforce whose wages and politics often brought violent conflict with the patrician elite. In the late fourteenth and fifteenth centuries the Flemish cloth industry declined under English competition, and the silting of the Zwin moved trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, but Flanders turned to lighter cloths, linen and tapestry and remained one of the richest regions of Europe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cassel-1071"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cassel-1071.jpg" alt="Battle of Cassel (1071)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cassel-1071">Battle of Cassel (1071)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Cassel,_22_February_1071_(Histoire_de_Belgiques,_1842).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre Olivier Joseph Coomans / Gustave Lesestre, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> was rarely a great military power in its own right, but its wealth and strategic position made it a battlefield. Count Robert I the Frisian won the county at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cassel-1071">Battle of Cassel</a> in 1071, and Flemish knights were prominent on the First Crusade under Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-count-of-artois">Robert II</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders">Philip of Alsace</a> died at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1191 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor">Baldwin IX</a> was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204. The French crown’s efforts to control Flanders led to repeated wars. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-ferrand">Ferrand</a> was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in 1214, fighting in the coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Otto IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II Augustus</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> occupied the county in 1300, the townsmen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matins-of-bruges">Bruges Matins</a> of May 1302, and on 11 July 1302 an army of Flemish militias, fighting on foot with pikes and the spiked club called the <em>goedendag</em>, destroyed the French cavalry at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took revenge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-en-pevele">Mons-en-Pévèle</a> in 1304, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassel-nord">Cassel</a> in 1328 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roosebeke">Roosebeke</a> in 1382. In the Hundred Years’ War the English fleet destroyed the French navy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sluys">Battle of Sluys</a> off the Flemish coast in 1340, and in later centuries southern Flanders was repeatedly fought over by France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-good.jpg" alt="Charles the Good" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sint-Salvatorskathedraal_-_Bruges_-_IMG_4774.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daderot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> built one of the most effective principalities of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They administered their lands through castellanies, each centred on a fortress and run by a castellan, and from the twelfth century increasingly through salaried bailiffs who could be dismissed. Comital revenues were audited centrally, and the account of 1187 known as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grote-brief">Grote Brief</a></em> is among the earliest surviving princely financial records of its kind in Europe. The murder of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> in the church of St Donatian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> in 1127, described in detail by the notary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galbert-of-bruges">Galbert of Bruges</a>, set off a succession crisis in which the towns for the first time claimed a say in choosing the count. The great towns enjoyed wide self-government under aldermen called <em>schepenen</em>, and by the fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, Bruges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>, joined later by the rural district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franc-of-bruges">Franc of Bruges</a>, formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-members-of-flanders">Four Members of Flanders</a>, which negotiated taxation with the count. From 1338 to 1345 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-van-artevelde">Jacob van Artevelde</a> of Ghent in effect governed much of the county in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Burgundian dukes added a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-flanders">Council of Flanders</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> in 1386, as the county’s high court. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> Flanders kept its own estates and law, and French suzerainty over it was renounced in 1526 and 1529.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ghent-altarpiece"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghent-altarpiece.jpg" alt="Ghent Altarpiece" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ghent-altarpiece">Ghent Altarpiece</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghent_Altarpiece_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan van Eyck / Presumably Hubert van Eyck, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>’ wealth gave it an influence far beyond its size. Its cloth industry made it, with northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, one of the two great manufacturing centres of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its towns pioneered forms of commercial life, from fairs and exchanges to urban self-government, that spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Its rebellions, above all the victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a>, showed that town militias on foot could defeat mounted knights, a lesson repeated by the Scots and Swiss in the following decades. Flanders was a hinge of politics between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>: English wool exports and Flemish alliances shaped the opening of the Hundred Years’ War, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III</a> was proclaimed king of France at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1340. Under the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Flanders became the core of a rich new state, and its painters, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-netherlandish-painting">Flemish Primitives</a>, transformed European art; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-eyck">Jan van Eyck</a> worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent-altarpiece">Ghent Altarpiece</a> was completed in 1432. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was born in Ghent in 1500. The county’s legacy also lies in its name. The northern, Dutch-speaking part of the old county became the core of the modern region of Flanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, whose name came to cover all of Dutch-speaking Belgium, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-flanders">French Flanders</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> remains part of France.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gavere"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gavere.jpg" alt="Battle of Gavere" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gavere">Battle of Gavere</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:15th-century_painters_-_Surrender_of_the_Burghers_of_Ghent_in_1453_-_WGA15789.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meister der Privilegien von Gent und Flandern, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent county ended with the extinction of the male line of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dampierre">Dampierre</a>. In 1369 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders">Margaret of Male</a>, heiress of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders">Louis of Male</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and on Louis’s death in 1384 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> became part of the Burgundian State. Philip and his successors crushed later urban revolts, most bloodily when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gavere">Gavere</a> in 1453. Through the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Austria</a> in 1477, Flanders passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and became one of the provinces of the Habsburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and then of the Spanish Netherlands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> split it: Ghent was a Calvinist republic from 1577 until its recapture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1584, and the strip south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> estuary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeelandic-flanders">Zeelandic Flanders</a>, became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, confirmed in 1648. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV of France</a> conquered the southern part in a series of wars, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> in 1668 and other towns by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> was bought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1662. The remaining county passed in 1714 to the Austrian Netherlands. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> of 1789–1790, French revolutionary armies occupied the region, and on 1 October 1795 the county was annexed by France and divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lys-department">Lys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/escaut-department">Escaut</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> was one of the two great manufacturing centres of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, whose cloth towns pioneered urban self-government, defeated French knights at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> in 1302, financed the Burgundian state and nurtured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-netherlandish-painting">Flemish Primitives</a>, leaving its name to the modern region of Flanders.</em></p>

